60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option B is a recipe for chaos. Without fixed spots, the morning rush will become a shoving match, and bikes will be damaged by poor placement. It ignores the physical reality of the space and the needs of daily riders.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option A is financially risky. While it adds to the shared account, it increases the administrative burden on the treasurer, who is already struggling. We must avoid any system that adds significant monthly billing work.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,"Option C provides a fair way to break the five-year stagnation I discovered. It removes the perception of favoritism and ensures that no single household holds a privileged spot indefinitely, all without adding to the treasurer's workload.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,Option C is just a slow-motion version of the current mess. We'd be trading a five-year grudge for a yearly lottery where the losers spend twelve months resentfully eyeing the winners' dry spots. It's a gamble with our social peace.,"{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mio,,"Option A sounds like a total nightmare for the treasurer! I don't want them spending all their free time on paperwork, and we can't have people skipping meetings because of money. Option B sounds a bit messy, but Option C is so simple!","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@George, while Option C addresses stagnation, it ignores the physical disparity of the spaces. A lottery doesn't solve the fact that some spots are wet and others are dry; it just randomizes who suffers. We need a more logical approach.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, while I agree that randomization isn't logical, Option B is also problematic. Installing double-tier racks would likely require a levy on households, as our ledger shows no funds for capital work. We cannot afford a costly installation.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Ryuji, that is a valid point regarding the costs. However, Option C requires no investment and no ongoing billing. It is the most stable way to ensure that the ""unspoken rules"" of the past five years are finally replaced by a transparent process.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, ""transparent"" doesn't mean ""peaceful."" A lottery just gives people a formal reason to be bitter. If we want to avoid the lobby becoming a cold war zone, we need something that doesn't feel like a game of chance.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Shunichi, you're always so worried about the fighting! But @George is right, Option C is super easy to run! No monthly bills, no expensive racks, just one big draw a year! It's the best way to keep the work low!","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Shunichi"",""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, ease of administration is not a substitute for a functional system. Option C is a superficial fix. It fails to address the physical damage caused by poor placement and the rain exposure, merely rotating the misfortune among residents.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, while I acknowledge the physical flaws, we must prioritize the building's constraints. Option A is an administrative burden and Option B is a financial impossibility. Option C is the only choice that is both cost-neutral and manageable.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,"@Asuka, while it does not solve the rain issue, it solves the social issue of perceived favoritism. A lottery is a neutral arbiter that everyone can accept, preventing the resentment that comes from stagnant, privileged spots.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@George, you're treating the residents like numbers in a spreadsheet. A ""neutral arbiter"" doesn't stop people from being petty. If we want to avoid a building where neighbors time their exits to avoid each other, we need a solution that doesn't feel like a lottery for misery.","{""words"":47,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Shunichi, you're making it sound so scary! But look, Option A is a paperwork monster and Option B costs too much money. Option C is the only one that doesn't turn into a giant chore for someone! I vote for Option C!","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, @Ryuji, @George, you are prioritizing convenience over the actual state of our property. Option C is a gamble that ignores the physical damage and rain exposure. However, Option A and B are untenable. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Mio"",""Ryuji"",""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I have analyzed the options against our constraints. Option A is an administrative nightmare and Option B is financially unfeasible. Option C is the only rational, cost-neutral choice that doesn't burden the treasurer. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,"I have considered the arguments. While it does not solve every physical flaw, Option C is the only way to fairly dismantle the long-term stagnation and perceived favoritism without creating a financial or administrative crisis. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"Since the rest of you are so eager to embrace the ""neutrality"" of a lottery, I'll stop trying to save you from yourselves. It's a mediocre solution, but it's better than a paperwork monster. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Mio,,Yay! I'm so glad we all agree! Option C is the only one that doesn't turn into a giant chore or a money disaster. Let's make the lottery a fun event! I vote for Option C!,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
